      "content": [
        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"flaubert-madame-bovary-9c9d14e73da47022\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":3,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.93,\"analysis\":\"Emma’s “je t’aime” primarily avows her consuming love for Rodolphe. It also has independently supported expressive force: the narration interprets such language as the possible overflowing of a full soul, making the words an affective discharge rather than merely an impassioned assertion. It does not itself clearly undertake a commitment or obligation.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The degree of E is somewhat uncertain because this is recurrent, conventionalized lover’s language rather than an explicitly involuntary blurt; nevertheless, the narrator’s overflow metaphor supplies unusually direct positive evidence for expressive force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"quoted_or_revoiced\",\"description\":\"Direct-discourse rendering by the narrator of Emma’s recurrent spoken response to Rodolphe, presented through iterative imperfect narration (“reprenait-elle”).\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Emma speaks amid jealousy, dependency, tears, and demands for reassurance. Rodolphe discounts her declarations because he has heard similar clichés from other lovers, but the narrator explicitly distinguishes sameness of expression from difference of feeling and suggests that Emma’s exaggerated, conventional metaphors can be the overflow of genuine inner plenitude. Her sincerity and emotional excess support T and E; her surrounding declarations of submission do not turn the highlighted love avowal itself into a clear relational undertaking.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“je t’aime à ne pouvoir me passer de toi”\",\"supports\":\"The continuation explicitly presents love as Emma’s consuming emotional state, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Emma describes longing, jealousy, and “toutes les colères de l’amour” tearing her apart.\",\"supports\":\"Her sustained account independently corroborates the loving and possessive affect avowed by the core utterance.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narrator contrasts Rodolphe’s skepticism with the possibility that “la plénitude de l’âme ne débordât pas quelquefois par les métaphores les plus vides.”\",\"supports\":\"The image of the soul overflowing through words positively supports expressive/reflexive force beyond mere passionate delivery.\",\"confidence\":0.95},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Il s’était tant de fois entendu dire ces choses” and Emma “reprenait-elle” situate the speech as recurrent lover’s discourse.\",\"supports\":\"Supports the quoted-or-revoiced iterative status and slightly limits E by showing that the language is recurrent and conventionalized rather than plainly involuntary.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Rodolphe “ne croyait que faiblement à la candeur” of Emma’s phrases, while the narrator says he failed to perceive “la dissemblance des sentiments sous la parité des expressions.”\",\"supports\":\"Shows that Rodolphe’s doubts concern sincerity and cliché, not a non-love-related core use; the narrative framing favors genuine feeling.\",\"confidence\":0.95}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T and E naturally capture the avowal of love and the narrator-supported overflowing of affect into language. The surrounding jealousy, dependency, cliché, and unequal relationship are contextual complexities rather than missing dimensions of the core force.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The classification concerns the highlighted second “je t’aime,” continued by “à ne pouvoir me passer de toi.”\"}"
        }
      ],
